load @term/seed/code/type/term
  find term

# The self type, the one primitive under all inductive types, the reason the trusted kernel stays tiny yet expresses
# inductive and inductive-inductive data (exactly what the self-hosting compiler needs for its own syntax tree). A self
# type binds a value to its own type. Its rules in full.

# FORMATION: from a body that is a type given a variable standing for the self type itself, form the self type. The
# variable refers to the very type being defined, which is what lets a type state its own induction principle.
form self-type
  link body, like term

# INTRODUCTION: a value inhabits the self type when it inhabits the body with the value itself substituted for the
# self-variable. This is a coercion the checker discharges by normalization.
form self-introduction
  link value, like term

# ELIMINATION: a value of the self type may be used at the body with the value substituted in, the inverse coercion.
# Introduction and elimination are mutually inverse, so the self type is definitionally equal to its unrolling.
form self-elimination
  link value, like term

# THE ENCODING PATTERN: every inductive type is a self type whose body is its own dependent eliminator. The booleans,
# the naturals, the integers, lists, and the compiler's own abstract syntax tree are all this one pattern (see the
# boolean and natural files). Nothing else is added as a primitive: the entire inductive world is self types.
form inductive-encoding
  link type, like self-type
